  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The critical point of the function $f(x)=(2x+1){e}^{x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1){e}^{x-2}) \\\\  & ={(2x+1)}^{\u2032}{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){({e}^{x-2})}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =2{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =[2+(2x+1)]{e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =(2x+3){e}^{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3){e}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x-2}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{x-2}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$",
